%N A183892 T(n,k)=Number of arrangements of n+3 numbers in 0..k with each number being the sum mod (k+1) of three others.
%C A183892 Every column satisfies a linear recurrence with constant coefficients. For column k, with q = k + 1 residues, T(n,k) = Sum_{j=1..q} P_j(n)*j^n for all sufficiently large n, where P_q(n) = q^3 and P_j is a polynomial of degree at most 3*(q - j). Moreover P_j = 0 for q/2 < j < q, and, if q is prime, for (q - 1)/3 + 1 <= j < q. Hence the g.f. of column k is rational with denominator Product_{j: P_j <> 0} (1 - j*x)^(deg(P_j) + 1). The recurrences of columns 1..8 have orders 3, 4, 7, 8, 12, 11, 16, 17. - _Mikhail Smirnov_, Nov 01 2026
%H A183892 R. H. Hardin, <a href="/A183892/b183892.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..176</a>
